---
import Decide from "../components/tools/ai-subscription/Decide.svelte";
import { LAST_CHECKED } from "../components/tools/ai-subscription/flow";
import { PLANS } from "../components/tools/ai-subscription/plans";
import ToolLayout from "../layouts/ToolLayout.astro";
---

<ToolLayout
  title="The best AI subscription for about $20"
  description="One answer per person, not a list of five. Prices include what you actually pay after tax, checked against each vendor's own page."
>
  <p class="lede">
    People ask me this constantly, so here is the answer as a flowchart instead
    of an essay. I pay for these and use them for code and writing, which is the
    only reason this page exists.
  </p>

  <Decide client:idle />

  <section class="plans">
    <h2>The five plans, side by side</h2>
    <ul>
      {
        PLANS.map((plan) => (
          <li>
            <div class="plan-head">
              <h3>
                <a href={plan.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                  {plan.name}
                </a>
              </h3>
              <p class="plan-price">{plan.price}</p>
            </div>
            <p class="plan-note">{plan.priceNote}</p>
            <p>{plan.headline}</p>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="notes">
    <h2>How I judge this</h2>
    <p>
      Usage resets are the thing nobody puts on a pricing page, and they change
      the answer more than model benchmarks do. When a vendor is handing out
      resets, the same $20 buys several times the work, and that is worth more
      than a slightly smarter model.
    </p>
    <p>
      Chat and coding limits are usually separate pools. That is why ChatGPT
      Plus keeps landing at the top for people who do both: it is two allowances
      for one price, not one allowance shared across everything.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you only take one thing from this page: the gap between a $10 plan and
      a $20 plan is much smaller than the gap between paying nothing and paying
      $10. Start cheap, find the limit, then upgrade when you actually hit it.
    </p>
    <p class="updated">Prices last checked {LAST_CHECKED}.</p>
  </section>
</ToolLayout>

<style>
  .lede {
    max-width: 36rem;
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--text-color);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .plans,
  .notes {
    margin-top: 3.5rem;
  }

  h2 {
    margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
    font-family: var(--font-family-serif);
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--heading-color);
  }

  .plans ul {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1.5rem;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .plans li {
    padding-left: 1rem;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--border-color);
  }

  .plan-head {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.35rem 0.75rem;
    align-items: baseline;
  }

  .plan-head h3 {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 1rem;
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .plan-price {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--gray-color);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  .plan-note {
    margin: 0.25rem 0 0.5rem;
    color: var(--gray-color);
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  .plans li > p:last-child {
    margin: 0;
    line-height: 1.55;
  }

  .notes p {
    max-width: 38rem;
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
  }

  .updated {
    color: var(--gray-color);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
  }
</style>
